# Key Learning
Network-based threat hunting requires correlating evidence across multiple log sources rather than relying on any single alert. The Snort alert provided the starting timestamp and the internal IP, but the full kill chain — dropper delivery, C2 beaconing, SMB discovery, RDP lateral movement, and data exfiltration — was only reconstructable by pivoting between Zeek HTTP, DNS, TLS, conn, and files logs alongside the FortiGate firewall records. JA4 fingerprinting of TLS connections provides a stable identifier for C2 client behaviour that persists even when the server-side infrastructure rotates. C2 commands encoded as hex then JSON then Base64 are a deliberate multi-layer obfuscation approach designed to evade both signature-based detection and casual inspection of application-layer traffic.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- Repeated short-duration outbound TLS connections from a developer workstation to an external IP on port 443 at regular intervals are a high-confidence beaconing indicator and should trigger automated isolation pending review
- A file downloaded from a domain mimicking a legitimate software distribution service (microsoftservice-style naming) should be sandboxed before execution, particularly when the download occurs outside business hours
- SMB connections from a single workstation to a large number of unique internal IPs in a short window are a textbook lateral movement discovery pattern and should alert immediately via network detection rules
- RDP from a developer workstation to an internal server not in its normal communication baseline is an anomalous lateral movement indicator requiring investigation regardless of whether credentials are valid
- Outbound transfers of large archives from an internal server to an external endpoint immediately following an unusual DNS resolution should be treated as a confirmed exfiltration event and trigger network-level blocking and data classification review
- C2 commands encoded across multiple layers (hex, JSON, Base64) are designed to evade shallow content inspection, network security monitoring must inspect and decode application-layer payloads rather than relying solely on connection metadata
